g factor and its modular\n160\t * inverse, and then scales each element of both matrices by their respective\n161\t * factors. The operation is performed modulo `mod`.\n162\t *\n163\t * @param m Pointer to the first matrix.\n164\t * @param invm Pointer to the second matrix.\n165\t * @param mod The modulus to apply to each element.\n166\t *\n167\t * @return 1 if successful (typo correction: should be 0 if successful).\n168\t */\n169\tint scale_transform(Matrix2D *m, Matrix2D *invm, uint64_t mod);\n170\t\n171\t/**\n172\t * @brief Set the given matrix to the identity matrix.\n173\t *\n174\t * This function sets the elements of the given matrix `m` to form an identity\n175\t * matrix. An identity matrix is a square matrix in which all elements are zero\n176\t * except for the diagonal elements, which are set to one.\n177\t *\n178\t * @param m Pointer to the matrix to be set to the identity matrix.\n179\t *\n180\t * @return 0 if successful.\n181\t */\n182\tint matrix2d_eye(Matrix2D *m);\n183\t\n184\t/**\n185\t * @brief Fill randomly generated invertible matrix pairs using a set of simple\n186\t * linear transformations.\n187\t *\n188\t * This function fills randomly generated invertible matrix pairs `m` and `invm`\n189\t * using a set of simple linear transformations. For each iteration, it randomly\n190\t * selects a transformation from the available set of transformations and\n191\t * applies it to both matrices `m` and `invm`.\n192\t *\n193\t * The set of available transformations includes swapping rows and columns,\n194\t * scaling each element by a random factor, and applying a linear mixing\n195\t * transformation.\n196\t *\n197\t * @param m Pointer to the first matrix.\n198\t * @param invm Pointer to the second matrix.\n199\t * @param mod The modulus to apply to each element.\n200\t * @param iterations The number of iterations to perform, each representing an\n201\t * application of a transformation.\n202\t */\n203\tint fill_random_invertible_pairs(Matrix2D *m, Ma